[Fact:theory-of-operation:Theory Of Operation]
The Currah μSpeech is a 2K ROM that occupies the same addresses
as the Spectrum ROM. Any access to #R$0038 (including the opcode
fetch that lands there) pages one or the other in. The Spectrum
IM1 handler always jumps to #R$0038, so this ROM runs first on
every interrupt; every exit jumps back to #R$0038 to page the
Spectrum ROM in and let IM1 finish.

The ROM is mirrored from #N$0800 to #N$0FFF. Speech itself is
produced by the #FACT(sp0256,SP0256) chip, fed one allophone at
a time from a short workspace just below
*#SKOOLKID$5CB2(RAMTOP).

#TABLE(default,centre)
{ =h Range | =h Contents }
{ #N$0000-#N$0037 | Banner bitmaps }
{ #N$0038-#N$03AE | Interrupt and helpers }
{ #N$03AF-#N$075A | Keys, suffixes and dictionary }
{ #N$075B-#N$0792 | Banner address list }
{ #N$0793-#N$0797 | "Anson" }
{ #N$0798-#N$07FF | More banner bitmaps }
{ #N$0800-#N$0FFF | Mirror of this ROM }
{ #N$1000 | #FACT(sp0256,SP0256) allophone / status }
{ #N$3000 / #N$3001 | Low / high intonation }
TABLE#

On the first few frames #R$0081 lowers *#SKOOLKID$5CB2(RAMTOP)
by one page and plants the workspace (low byte #N$57). After
that, each interrupt at #R$00B7:

#LIST
{ Ticks *#SKOOLKID$5C78(FRAMES), because this ROM owns IM1 }
{ Ticks the two keyboard-state timer pairs }
{ Expands the BASIC string #FACT(sstring,s$) if it exists }
{ Speaks a new key from *#SKOOLKID$5C08(LAST_K) if keys is #N$01 }
{ Sends the next queued allophone if the #FACT(sp0256,SP0256) is idle }
LIST#

Letters, tokens and symbols become allophone IDs through
#R$0447. A parenthesised name such as "(SH)" picks an allophone
directly (see #R$02EB). The queued IDs sit in the workspace
buffer and grow downwards; when the #FACT(sp0256,SP0256) is
ready, #R$0166 writes one ID and shuffles the rest down.

[Fact:workspace:Speech Workspace]
The workspace is a few bytes in the page of
*#SKOOLKID$5CB4(P_RAMT). #R$0354 reads index #N$00 as the byte
at low address #N$57, index #N$01 as #N$56, and so on. A 48K
machine uses #N$FF57; a 16K machine uses #N$7F57.
#R$0081 lowers *#SKOOLKID$5CB2(RAMTOP) by one page so BASIC
stays out of this block.

#TABLE(default,centre,centre)
{ =h Index | =h Address | =h Purpose }
{ #N$00 | #N$57 | Flags }
{ #N$01 | #N$56 | Cleared on install }
{ #N$03 | #N$54 | Live buffer pointer (low, then high) }
{ #N$06 | #N$51 | Saved queue start / first allophone }
TABLE#

The live pointer is a word, so its high byte sits at #N$55
(install first writes the workspace page there). The allophone
buffer begins at the address of index #N$06 and grows down
towards the new *#SKOOLKID$5CB2(RAMTOP). #R$0375 sets carry
when the pointer has dropped into that reserved page.

Flags at index #N$00:

#TABLE(default,centre)
{ =h Bit | =h Meaning }
{ 1 | Key speech (set when keys is #N$01) }
{ 2 | Workspace live (set while installing, reset when ready) }
{ 4 | A key is already being spoken }
{ 6 | High intonation (set for uppercase, reset for lowercase) }
{ 7 | Banner not yet drawn }
TABLE#

[Fact:sp0256:SP0256 Speech Synthesiser]
The μSpeech speaks through a General Instrument SP0256-AL2
chip. #R$0166 feeds it one allophone at a time from the
workspace queue.

#N$1000 is the allophone port. Reading it returns status; bit 0
set means the chip is still speaking. Writing an allophone ID
starts that sound; writing #N$00 silences the chip.

Intonation is selected first by writing the same ID to #N$3000
(low) or #N$3001 (high). Bit 6 of the queued byte chooses the
port; #R$017F then writes the ID to #N$1000.

The IDs themselves are the AL2 allophone set stored in #R$0447.
Hear every ID on the #LINK(Sound)(Allophones) page.

[Fact:sstring:BASIC Speech String]
Programs speak by assigning the BASIC string variable s$. On each
interrupt #R$00DD looks that name up with #R$01CD (name code #N$53)
and, if it exists, calls #R$021F.

#R$021F ignores an empty string, and also ignores a string whose
first character is "*" or "?". Those two markers mean the text has
already been spoken, or could not be parsed.

Anything else is expanded into the allophone buffer. A full stop
queues a short pause. A "(" starts a parenthesised allophone name
(see #R$02EB). Other characters are mapped through #R$03AF.

After a successful pass the first character of s$ is overwritten
with "*". If a character cannot be mapped, it is overwritten with
"?" instead. Either way the next interrupt will skip the string
until a program assigns s$ again.

The separate numeric variable keys (#N$01) enables speaking each
key press from *#SKOOLKID$5C08(LAST_K); that path does not use s$.

The #LINK(TryIt)(Try s$) page runs this parser in the browser
and plays the resulting #FACT(sp0256,SP0256) allophones.

[Fact:punctuation:Key Speech Remap]
When a new key arrives in *#SKOOLKID$5C08(LAST_K), #R$0100 turns it
into a dictionary key and looks it up in #R$0447. Letters are
uppercased, and codes #N$80-#N$A4 (block graphics) all use key
#N$80. Everything else is offered to #R$042D first.

#R$011D searches the #N$0D keys at #R$042D. A miss keeps the original
code. A hit takes the byte the same distance into #R$043A and uses
that as the dictionary key instead.

The table covers symbols and tokens that either share a spoken word
with another key, or have no entry of their own in #R$0447 (POINT,
CLS, SGN and AND).

#TABLE(default,centre,centre)
{ =h LAST_K | =h Dictionary key }
{ ")" | "(" }
{ "[" | "(" }
{ "]" | "(" }
{ "{" | "(" }
{ "}" | "(" }
{ POINT | "." }
{ CLS | CLEAR }
{ "@" | AT }
{ Cursor right | Cursor (#N$08) }
{ Cursor down | Cursor (#N$08) }
{ Cursor up | Cursor (#N$08) }
{ SGN | SIN }
{ AND | "&" }
TABLE#

All brackets therefore share the "(" entry (spoken as a bracket).
The extra cursor keys share the spoken "cursor" for #N$08. POINT is
spoken as a full stop, CLS as CLEAR, "@" as AT, SGN as SIN, and AND
as "&".

[Fact:dictionary:Allophone Dictionary]
#R$0447 is the character-to-allophone table used by #R$012C. Each
entry is a key byte followed by one or more #FACT(sp0256,SP0256-AL2)
allophone IDs. Bit 6 of an ID selects high intonation; bit 7 marks
the last ID of that entry.

The table starts with a dummy terminator (#N$80) so the scanner's
"end of the previous list" step lands on the first real key, and it
ends on a key of #N$00.

#LIST
{ Control keys: EDIT, ENTER, cursor, DELETE }
{ Colour tokens, graphics and symbols, plus LET and NEW }
{ Digits #N$30-#N$39 }
{ "#", "$" and "%" }
{ Letters A-Z }
{ BASIC tokens from RND through COPY }
LIST#

Each comment line on #R$0447 has an audio player for that
spoken list.

A key that is not in the table is skipped; the interrupt then
continues to #R$0166. That is why POINT, CLS, SGN and AND are
rewritten by #R$042D before the lookup.

[Fact:suffixes:Allophone Name Suffixes]
A parenthesised name in #FACT(sstring,s$) such as "(AA)" or "(SH)"
is read by #R$02EB. Each letter is normalised by #R$035E (so "A"
and "a" both become #N$21) and must appear in the first #N$18
entries of #R$03AF.
Those values are summed; the sum is the lookup key.

Most sums are searched for in the #N$36 bytes from #R$03AF, and the
matching allophone is #N$35 bytes later. Five sums are handled
separately and go through the suffix tables instead:

#TABLE(default,centre,centre)
{ =h Sum | =h Letter used }
{ #N$64 | Last letter (e.g. "(RR)") }
{ #N$68 | Last letter (e.g. "(OY)") }
{ #N$5C | Last letter (e.g. "(NN)") }
{ #N$66 | Last letter (e.g. "(OW)") }
{ #N$78 | Letter before last (e.g. "(EAR)") }
TABLE#

#R$0336 takes that letter, normalises it, and searches #R$041B. The
parallel byte #N$08 later in #R$0424 is the allophone. A letter that
is not in #R$041B makes the group invalid.

#TABLE(default,centre,centre)
{ =h Suffix letter | =h Allophone }
{ "R" | #N$0E (RR1) }
{ "U" | #N$16 (UW1) }
{ "T" | #N$0D (TT2) }
{ "Y" | #N$05 (OY) }
{ "H" | #N$1D (TH) }
{ "N" | #N$38 (NN2) }
{ "A" | #N$3C (YR) }
{ "E" | #N$2F (XR) }
{ "W" | #N$20 (AW) }
TABLE#
